  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1019 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nLittle to no precipitation chances are expected over the next week \nor so. The main issue in the long term forecast will be several \npossible days of windy conditions. A progressive upper level pattern \nwill primarily be responsible for this windy pattern. The first of \nthese windy days will occur on Saturday as a short wave trough moves \nacross the Intermountain West emerging onto the Central Plains \nSaturday evening. Winds aloft will be increasing throughout the day \nwith 500mb winds peaking around 70kt late afternoon with 700mb winds \nup to 40kt. However, boundary layer mixing is not expected to be \nincredibly deep on Saturday with mixing up to 800 to 750mb. This \nshortwave will be able to generate strong lee cyclogenesis in \neastern Colorado increasing the pressure gradient locally across \nWest Texas. The strongest surface winds are expected to be over the \nextreme southwestern Texas Panhandle with speeds possibly exceeding \nadvisory criteria. These strong downsloping westerly winds will also \nbe able to boost temperatures well above seasonal averages in the \nrange of 10 to 20 degrees above average. A strong cold front will \nfollow Saturday evening swinging temperatures drastically in the \nopposite direction. A weaker short wave trough is then forecast to \nmove out of the Rockies on Monday bringing another windy day but not \nto the degree expected for Saturday. Another repeat is then possible \nfor Wednesday.",
